  "worked_solution": "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=x+4$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} & =34 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(x+4-5)^{2} & =34 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(x-1)^{2} & =34 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+{x}^{2}-2x+1 & =34 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8x-24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-12 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-2,6$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\  & =-2+4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-2,2).$ Next, we plug in $x=6$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\  & =6+4 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(6,10).$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=x+4$ intersects the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-5)^{2}=34$ at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(6,10).$",
